      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a coordinate plane, we use the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\nGiven the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\), we can substitute \\(x_1 = -3\\), \\(y_1 = -4\\), \\(x_2 = 4\\), and \\(y_2 = -5\\) into the formula. Let's calculate it step by step:\n\n1. Compute \\(x_2 - x_1\\):\n\\[\nx_2 - x_1 = 4 - (-3) = 4 + 3 = 7\n\\]\n\n2. Compute \\(y_2 - y_1\\):\n\\[\ny_2 - y_1 = -5 - (-4) = -5 + 4 = -1\n\\]\n\n3. Square the differences:\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_1)^2 = 7^2 = 49\n\\]\n\\[\n(y_2 - y_1)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\n4. Add the squared differences:\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2 = 49 + 1 = 50\n\\]\n\n5. Take the square root of the sum:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = \\sqrt{25} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is \\(\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\\) units.",
